1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,234
This tutorial follows the contract from
tutorial 47 into the books.

2
00:00:04,234 --> 00:00:08,468
Open Rajesh Kumar's contract for A-202,
and select View posting journal.

3
00:00:08,468 --> 00:00:10,007
Posting wrote journal TCO-26/7.

4
00:00:10,007 --> 00:00:13,087
Rent Receivable is debited with each part
of

5
00:00:13,087 --> 00:00:16,166
the contract, 47,000 in all: what Rajesh
owes.

6
00:00:16,166 --> 00:00:20,785
Against it, the rent of 42,000 is credited
to Advance Rent, not

7
00:00:20,785 --> 00:00:25,404
to income, because rent is earned day by
day over the term.

8
00:00:25,404 --> 00:00:28,868
The deposit of 4,000 is credited to
Security Deposit:

9
00:00:28,868 --> 00:00:32,332
it is held for Rajesh, and is never
income.

10
00:00:32,332 --> 00:00:36,951
The admin fee of 1,000 is income the day
it is charged.

11
00:00:36,951 --> 00:00:40,030
Back on the contract, the Cheques tab
lists

12
00:00:40,030 --> 00:00:43,109
every journal it has written, under
Journal Vouchers.

13
00:00:43,109 --> 00:00:45,803
Posting also wrote one PDR for each

14
00:00:45,803 --> 00:00:48,113
of the six cheques in hand.

15
00:00:48,113 --> 00:00:51,192
Each moves its amount from Rent Receivable
to

16
00:00:51,192 --> 00:00:53,886
PDC Receivable: the cheques the landlord
holds.

17
00:00:53,886 --> 00:00:54,656
Open CRT-26/12.

18
00:00:54,656 --> 00:00:56,966
Clearing the deposit cheque wrote
CRT-26/12.

19
00:00:56,966 --> 00:01:01,200
The building's bank account is debited
4,000, and PDC Receivable is

20
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:05,433
credited the same: the cheque is now money
in the bank.

21
00:01:05,433 --> 00:01:09,283
Below the list are the same entries,
account by account.

22
00:01:09,283 --> 00:01:13,516
Advance Rent holds the full 42,000, none
of it earned yet.

23
00:01:13,516 --> 00:01:15,826
The bank holds the 4,000 deposit.

24
00:01:15,826 --> 00:01:19,290
PDC Receivable holds 43,000: the cheques
still to clear.

25
00:01:19,290 --> 00:01:22,754
And Rent Receivable is back to zero: with
every

26
00:01:22,754 --> 00:01:25,833
cheque in hand, Rajesh owes nothing on
account.

27
00:01:25,833 --> 00:01:29,297
Revenue recognition shows how the rent
will be earned:

28
00:01:29,297 --> 00:01:32,377
twelve monthly slices, worked out by the
day.

29
00:01:32,377 --> 00:01:36,226
At each month-end, that month's slice
moves from Advance Rent

30
00:01:36,226 --> 00:01:40,075
to Rental Income: 3,452.05 for the thirty
days of November.

31
00:01:40,075 --> 00:01:43,154
The last month absorbs the rounding, and
the

32
00:01:43,154 --> 00:01:45,848
schedule matches the contract rent of
42,000.

33
00:01:45,848 --> 00:01:49,697
Nobody posts these by hand: every night,
each month that

34
00:01:49,697 --> 00:01:53,161
has ended is posted on its own, and its

35
00:01:53,161 --> 00:01:56,626
row turns from Planned to Posted, with its
journal.

36
00:01:56,626 --> 00:01:59,705
Tutorial 51 shows it happening on these
contracts,

37
00:01:59,705 --> 00:02:02,784
and how to check that nothing was missed.
